# Reproducible object and archive fixtures

`generate.py` consumes a JSON catalog. The catalog names versioned source files,
source language, target triple, generated object path, and explicit archive
recipes. It compiles those inputs; it does not synthesize product fixture
sources. A later fixture expansion can add relocation inputs by extending the
catalog without changing the generator.

Supported triples are `x86_64-linux-gnu`, `i386-linux-gnu`,
`aarch64-linux-gnu`, `armv7-linux-gnueabihf`, `riscv64-linux-gnu`,
`powerpc64le-linux-gnu`, `x86_64-apple-macos11`, `arm64-apple-macos11`, and
`x86_64-w64-windows-gnu`. Object generation does not claim target runtime or
relocation-handler qualification.

The catalog schema is intentionally small. `sources` is an array of entries
with `id`, `path`, `language` (`c` or `asm`), `triple`, and `object`. `archives`
is an array with output `path`, `format` (`gnu`, `bsd`, or `thin`), and member
source IDs. Source paths are relative to the catalog directory. Object paths
must share a directory with their archive members so thin archives keep
portable relative references.

Use LLVM 17 or newer. Pass paths explicitly when the environment has compiler
wrappers that add target-inappropriate flags:

```sh
python3 generate.py regen --catalog /path/to/catalog.json \
  --output-dir /path/to/generated --clang /path/to/unwrapped/clang \
  --llvm-mc /path/to/llvm-mc --llvm-ar /path/to/llvm-ar
python3 generate.py check --catalog /path/to/catalog.json \
  --output-dir /path/to/generated --clang /path/to/unwrapped/clang \
  --llvm-mc /path/to/llvm-mc --llvm-ar /path/to/llvm-ar
```

Keep generated output in a directory separate from the catalog and its source
tree. The output directory must be empty or a tree previously created by this
generator. Regeneration builds into a temporary tree before replacing managed
files, so a tool failure leaves existing fixtures intact. `check` never writes
to expected data. It fails on missing or unknown output files, changed catalog
sources, or different artifact bytes. A tool-major mismatch emits a warning;
source or byte differences still fail. The manifest records only relative paths,
SHA-256 hashes, sizes, and tool versions.

Run the tests with:

```sh
python3 -m unittest discover -s liblink/tests/fixtures -p 'test_*.py'
```

The test inputs live only in temporary directories. Tool locations can be set
with `LL_FIXTURE_CLANG`, `LL_FIXTURE_LLVM_MC`, and `LL_FIXTURE_LLVM_AR`.

Two opt-in recipes cover additional fixture encodings. They run inside the
temporary generation tree and never patch product binaries. The helper
`fixture_recipes.py` is SHA-256-bound as `recipe_sha256` in every manifest
and compared strictly by `check`.

- A source entry may set `coff_rel32_variant` to an integer 1 through 5, only
  for `asm` with target `x86_64-w64-windows-gnu`. LLVM 21.1.2's
  `llvm/lib/Target/X86/MCTargetDesc/X86WinCOFFObjectWriter.cpp` emits plain
  `IMAGE_REL_AMD64_REL32`. The recipe requires exactly one such relocation
  in an ordinary AMD64 COFF object, changes its type to `REL32_N`, and adds
  N to its signed inline addend. Thus `S + A - (P + 4)` equals
  `S + (A + N) - (P + 4 + N)`. Versioned assembly must supply at least N
  bytes after the four-byte relocation field. Other bytes remain unchanged;
  bounds, symbol indices, overlapping regions and addend overflow are checked.
- A GNU archive entry may set `symbol_table_bits` to `64`. The generator
  invokes deterministic `llvm-ar` with a per-command `SYM64_THRESHOLD=0`
  environment and requires a leading `/SYM64/` index. LLVM's
  `llvm/lib/Object/ArchiveWriter.cpp` exposes this test knob to generate
  small 64-bit-index archives without multi-gigabyte inputs. There is no
  custom archive-byte rewriting. Other archive recipes explicitly use the
  default threshold of 2^32, overriding any ambient value. Tests verify
  widened big-endian counts/offsets, preserved member bytes and symbol names,
  plus independent `llvm-ar`/`llvm-nm` reader results.

Malformed or unsupported recipe inputs fail before output promotion. These
recipes supply format fixtures, including expected-unsupported relocations;
LLVM reader acceptance does not establish liblink backend support.
